{"ts":"2026-11-12T15:46:55.220Z","actor":"claude","stage":"factcheck","action":"factcheck.review","details":{"verdict":"pass","notes":"Semantic review: every spoken and on-screen number traces to a sourced or computed fact. Distances: NASA (Sagittarius A* at 26,000 light-years; Andromeda at 2.5 million light-years). Trip times: computed with hyperbolic motion for a constant 1 g accelerate-to-midpoint-and-brake profile (ship 19.8 years one way, 39.5 round trip to the centre; Earth 26,002 and 52,004 years; Andromeda round trip 57.2 ship years and 5,000,004 Earth years), so the spoken figures are rounded from those. The 13,400 factor is the Lorentz factor at the midpoint of the centre trip. The paradox line is the standard resolution: the traveller accelerates and turns around, so the traveller is younger (Wikipedia). The profile is idealised, and the scene and footnote say so; no ship can do this yet."},"gitSha":"ff27824"}
